<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="pack-updates"
      updated="August 4, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"record", "1. See where you are"},
        {"review", "2. Read what changed"},
        {"canary", "3. Update one runner"},
        {"trust", "4. Trust it and prove it"},
        {"fleet", "5. Roll through the fleet"},
        {"rollback", "6. Going back"},
        {"retirement", "Retired versions"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="pack-updates">
        <:dek>
          A pack update changes what your runners can execute, so it lands in two steps:
          install the new version on the runner, then trust it for dispatch. This page rolls out
          with a canary and includes the way back. A pack update never touches the runner binary
          — that is <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/runner-upgrades"}
            class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Upgrade runners</.link>.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>Admin or owner, to review and trust a pack version for the account.</:item>
        <:item>
          Root or sudo on each runner host — packs live under
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/etc/emisar/packs</code>
          and the tree is owned by root.
        </:item>
        <:item>One non-critical runner to update first.</:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <.docs_h2 id="record">1. See where you are</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Start from the host — that is where the packs are. List what is installed, see what
        the registry would change, and write down the id, version, and hash of the pack you are
        about to update: that line is your way back. Note which runners or groups carry it.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500"># id, version, action count, and content hash per installed pack</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar pack list

<span class="text-zinc-500"># what an update would change — no changes made</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar pack update --dry-run

<span class="text-zinc-500">  redis                  v0.2.3 → v0.2.4 (update available)</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">  linux-core             up to date (v0.4.1)</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">  billing-tools          not in registry — left as-is</span>

<span class="text-zinc-500">  1 to update, 1 up to date, 1 not in registry, 0 failed.</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">  Review a change before applying: emisar pack diff &lt;id&gt;</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">  Run without --dry-run to apply.</span></.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">--dry-run</code>
        reports the plan and changes nothing. A pack the registry does not carry — one you
        authored yourself — is reported and left alone.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The console's Packs page shows the account view: each version, its trusted hash, and
        the hash each runner advertises.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="review">2. Read what changed</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Before you update, read what changed in the action contracts — LLM clients and
        runbooks depend on them. The runner shows the exact changes without touching
        anything:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500"># the changed lines between the installed pack and the registry's version</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar pack diff redis

<span class="text-zinc-500"># against a specific published version, or the summary alone</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar pack diff redis --to 0.2.4
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar pack diff redis --stat</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The candidate is fetched and hash-verified the same way an update fetches it, and
        the diff covers the files behind the pack's content hash — <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">pack.yaml</code>, its action
        declarations, and its scripts. Read it for the changes that break callers: an
        action removed or renamed fails any runbook step that names it, and an argument's
        name, type, default, or validation changes what callers can send. Changes that
        alter what the host may do are called out above the diff — an escalated risk tier
        your policy may now route through approval, a dropped redaction rule, a changed
        execution user, a widened path allowlist, or a newly required setup variable.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/action-packs"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Pack reference</.link>
        explains the full action schema and each field, and the pack's page on
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/packs"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >the registry</.link>
        lists its declared actions and versions.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="canary">3. Update one runner</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Name the packs you mean rather than updating everything at once, and do it on one
        non-critical runner first.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500"># move one pack to the registry's current version</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar pack update redis

<span class="text-zinc-500"># or pin an exact published version and its hash</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar pack install redis=0.2.4 --hash sha256:… --force</.docs_code>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">The new version is verified before it goes live.</strong>
            The tarball is fetched, validated, and hash-checked against the registry index, staged
            beside the live pack, and only then swapped in. An activation failure restores the
            previous version immediately, and the staged swap keeps a backup beside the pack — after
            a power loss mid-swap, the next install or update recovers it.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">A running runner reloads itself.</strong>
            The command signals the daemon so it re-reads the catalog and re-advertises. No
            restart, and runs in flight are not dropped.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Several packs update independently.</strong>
            Each one succeeds or fails on its own. The command prints per-pack lines and a count,
            and exits non-zero if any failed. A partial success is a normal outcome — read the
            lines, not just the exit code.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="trust">4. Trust it and prove it</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Installing is not trusting: the files are on the host, and account trust decides what
        may run. The canary now advertises the new version. When its hash is one the published
        catalog carries,
        <a
          href={~p"/app/packs"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Packs</a>
        already shows the version as trusted. Skip to the check below.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Anything else — a pack
        you <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/publishing-packs"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >authored</.link>, one you edited, or a retired version — shows as
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">pending</strong>
        with the exact hash it is advertising, and dispatch for it is refused.
        Trust binds one exact content hash: check the pending hash against the one
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar pack list</code>
        printed on the host, then review and trust that version. Trusting records the complete
        action set for that exact version, so any later change is caught.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Run one low-risk read-only action against the canary. Confirm that it succeeds and appears
        in Audit. If a published runbook uses this pack, run its preflight before widening the
        rollout. Contract changes then appear as broken steps before production work starts.
      </p>
      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="Reject is the other answer">
        <p>
          Rejecting a pending version keeps the trusted one active. emisar remembers the
          rejected hash, so the same version never asks again — only a new one does.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="fleet">5. Roll through the fleet</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Repeat the update on bounded runner groups. Account trust is bound to the hash. Another
        runner with the trusted hash needs no second decision. A <em>different</em>
        hash for that version is drift. It stops and asks for review. After each group, confirm the
        advertised versions and hashes match what you trusted and run one representative action.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Group and label mechanics are in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/runner-fleet"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Manage the runner fleet</.link>. Batch sizing for a production fleet is in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/production"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Go to production</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="rollback">6. Going back</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        There is no rollback command — install the exact earlier version again.
        A published version is immutable and the registry is append-only, so the earlier
        version is still there, at the same address with the same hash.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500"># the version and hash you recorded before the update</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar pack install redis=0.2.3 --hash sha256:… --force</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">--hash</code>
        pin makes this exact: the install aborts unless the computed hash matches. Each
        version carries its own trust decision, so a previously trusted version resumes dispatch
        without another review, and a catalog hash needs none either. A version the catalog has
        dropped, or any hash the account has never trusted, lands as pending.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Do this per runner, in the same bounded groups you rolled forward in, and verify the same
        way. Remove an unwanted pack with <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar pack uninstall</code>. The command reloads the runner and removes those actions from its catalog.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="retirement">Retired versions</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        When a published pack ships a security or critical-correctness fix, it declares a
        retirement floor and every version below it is retired. A runner still advertising a
        retired version fails closed at dispatch with a message telling the operator to update the
        pack. An admin can deliberately re-trust a retired version in <a
          href={~p"/app/packs"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Packs</a>. That override
        is audited, and revoking trust clears it so a later re-trust has to decide again.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Floors move upward at publish time. The build that produces a catalog refuses to lower
            or drop a floor already in it, so a routine republish cannot quietly un-retire a
            version.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Retirement arrives with the catalog refresh. The control plane refreshes its pack
            catalog every ten minutes and keeps the last accepted snapshot after a failure; a new
            floor applies after a successful refresh.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Retirement blocks dispatch, not installation — a retired version stays installable,
            and nothing here deletes a published version or erases the history of a run that
            used it. Check the retirement floor before rolling back that far.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        If you maintain the pack yourself, publishing, versioning, and declaring a retirement floor
        are in
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/publishing-packs"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">Author your own pack</.link>
        and <.link navigate={~p"/docs/pack-registry"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">Host your own registry</.link>.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
